CAT = "melecGeneral"; SEG = "GEN"
Q = [
("A balanced 150 kVA three-phase load is supplied at 480 V. About what is the line current?",
 "About 180 A", ["About 312 A", "About 104 A", "About 90 A"],
 "Three-phase current is VA divided by (volts x 1.732): 150,000 / (480 x 1.732) is about 180 A. Leaving out the 1.732 gives the 312 A figure."),
("A 208 V three-phase feeder of 4 AWG copper (41,740 cmil, K = 12.9) carries 50 A for 200 ft. About what is the voltage drop?",
 "About 5.4 V", ["About 6.2 V", "About 3.1 V", "About 10.7 V"],
 "Three-phase drop is 1.732 x K x I x L / cmil: 1.732 x 12.9 x 50 x 200 / 41,740 is about 5.4 V. The single-phase formula, with 2 in place of 1.732, gives about 6.2 V."),
("A load uses 90 kW at a power factor of 0.75. What apparent power must the supply deliver?",
 "120 kVA", ["67.5 kVA", "90 kVA", "150 kVA"],
 "Apparent power is true power divided by power factor: 90 / 0.75 = 120 kVA. Multiplying instead gives 67.5 kVA, which is less than the true power and cannot be right."),
("A 500 kVA, 480 V three-phase transformer has 5 percent impedance. About what fault current is available at its secondary on an infinite bus?",
 "About 12,000 A", ["About 6,000 A", "About 20,800 A", "About 30,100 A"],
 "Full-load current is 500,000 / (480 x 1.732), about 601 A, and the infinite-bus fault current is that divided by the impedance: 601 / 0.05 is about 12,000 A."),
("A dry-type transformer stepping 480 V down to 208Y/120 V, with no direct connection between primary and secondary, supplies a:",
 "Separately derived system", ["Service lateral", "Feeder tap", "Service-entrance conductors"],
 "Article 100 defines a separately derived system as a premises system whose power comes from a source such as a transformer, with no direct connection to the supply conductors except through grounding and bonding."),
("The highest current at rated voltage that a breaker is identified to interrupt is its:",
 "Interrupting rating", ["Continuous current rating", "Short-time rating", "Trip setting"],
 "Article 100 defines the interrupting rating as the highest current at rated voltage that a device is identified to interrupt. Article 110 requires it to be enough for the current available at the line terminals."),
("Besides the available fault current itself, the field marking on commercial service equipment must show:",
 "The date it was calculated", ["The installer's license number", "The utility transformer size", "The arc-flash boundary"],
 "Article 110 requires service equipment in other than dwelling units to be marked with the available fault current and the date the calculation was performed. The marking must be updated when changes increase the fault current."),
("A detailed arc-flash label on large commercial service equipment must state the nominal voltage, the available fault current and:",
 "The OCPD clearing time", ["The panel's weight", "The feeder conductor sizes", "The bonding jumper size"],
 "Article 110 requires the label to give the nominal system voltage, the available fault current at the service overcurrent device, its clearing time at that fault current, and the date the label was applied."),
("On an electrical floor plan, a small circle with two short parallel lines through it marks a:",
 "Duplex receptacle", ["Single receptacle", "Junction box", "Ceiling luminaire"],
 "The standard plan symbol for a duplex receptacle outlet is a circle crossed by two parallel lines. One line marks a single receptacle, and a circle with a J marks a junction box."),
("On a lighting plan, a switch marked \"S3\" is a:",
 "Three-way switch", ["Switch on circuit 3", "Switch mounted 3 ft up", "Three-pole breaker"],
 "Plan symbols put the switch type after the S: S3 is a three-way switch and S4 a four-way. Circuit numbers are shown on the home run, not in the switch symbol."),
("Beside a receptacle symbol on a plan, the letters \"WP\" mean:",
 "Weatherproof", ["Wall plate", "Wire pull", "Wire-pulling point"],
 "WP is the standard plan abbreviation for a weatherproof device or enclosure. Outdoor and wet-location receptacles are commonly marked this way."),
("Resistors of 12, 6 and 4 ohms are connected in parallel. What is the total resistance?",
 "2 ohms", ["22 ohms", "4 ohms", "7.3 ohms"],
 "Add the conductances: 1/12 + 1/6 + 1/4 = 0.5, and 1 / 0.5 = 2 ohms. A parallel total is always less than the smallest branch, so 4 ohms is too high."),
("A current of 40 A flows through a circuit whose conductors total 0.25 ohm. How much power is lost as heat in the conductors?",
 "400 W", ["10 W", "160 W", "1,600 W"],
 "Conductor loss is I squared x R: 40 x 40 x 0.25 = 400 W. Multiplying current by resistance gives only the 10 V drop, not the loss."),
("A 10 hp motor (7,460 W output) draws 8,800 W from the supply. About what is its efficiency?",
 "About 85 percent", ["About 76 percent", "About 92 percent", "About 118 percent"],
 "Efficiency is output divided by input: 7,460 / 8,800 is about 0.85, or 85 percent. Dividing input by output gives 118 percent, which is impossible for a motor."),
("A balanced three-phase resistive load draws 60 A per line at 208 V. About what is its true power?",
 "About 21.6 kW", ["About 12.5 kW", "About 37.4 kW", "About 7.2 kW"],
 "Three-phase power at unity power factor is 1.732 x V x I: 1.732 x 208 x 60 is about 21,600 W, or 21.6 kW. Volts times amps alone gives 12.5 kW."),
("Capacitors added to a plant with many induction motors mainly:",
 "Raise the power factor", ["Lower the true power", "Raise the voltage drop", "Raise the kvar from the utility"],
 "Capacitors supply the reactive power the motors need, so less kvar comes from the utility and the power factor rises. The true power used by the motors does not change."),
("In a four-wire wye system, which harmonic currents add together in the neutral instead of canceling?",
 "Third and other triplens", ["Fifth and other odd orders", "Seventh and eleventh", "Second and fourth"],
 "Triplen harmonics (3rd, 9th, 15th) are in phase on all three lines, so they add in the shared neutral. That is why nonlinear loads can overload a wye neutral."),
("Under Article 100, a qualified person has skills and knowledge of the equipment and has also:",
 "Had training on its hazards", ["Held a master's license", "Worked five years in the trade", "Passed a manufacturer's course"],
 "A qualified person has the skills and knowledge related to the construction and operation of the equipment and has received safety training to recognize and avoid the hazards involved."),
("Running equipment above its normal full-load rating, with no fault present, is called:",
 "Overload", ["Short circuit", "Ground fault", "Arc fault"],
 "Article 100 defines overload as operation in excess of normal full-load rating that would cause damage if it persists. A short circuit or ground fault is a fault, not an overload."),
("In the 2023 NEC, Chapter 8 communications systems are subject to Chapters 1 through 7:",
 "Only where referenced", ["Always, without exception", "Never, in any installation", "Only in dwellings"],
 "Article 90 of the 2023 NEC says Chapter 8 stands on its own and is not subject to Chapters 1 through 7 unless a Chapter 8 rule specifically refers to them. The 2026 NEC folds communications into Chapter 7."),
]
